  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Differentiate each function then determine whether the curve is increasing, decreasing, or stationary at $x=1$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=(x-5)(x+3)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=2x-2$; $y'(1)=0$, so stationary.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^2-2x-15\\\\y'&=2x-2\\\\y'(1)&=2(1)-2\\\\y'(1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is stationary at $x=1$ because $y'(1)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=(x-2)(x^2+5)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=3x^2-4x+5$; $y'(1)=4$, so increasing.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^3-2x^2+5x-10\\\\y'&=3x^2-4x+5\\\\y'(1)&=3(1)^2-4(1)+5\\\\y'(1)&=4>0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is increasing at $x=1$ because $y'(1)>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=(x^4+2)(1-x^3)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=4x^3-7x^6-6x^2$; $y'(1)=-9$, so decreasing.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=x^4-x^7+2-2x^3\\\\y'&=4x^3-7x^6-6x^2\\\\y'(1)&=4(1)^3-7(1)^6-6(1)^2\\\\y'(1)&=4-7-6\\\\y'(1)&=-9<0\\end{align*}$$\nThe curve is decreasing at $x=1$ because $y'(1)<0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
